# Mutual exclusion: from Peterson to hardware

Requirements for a critical-section solution: mutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting.

## Peterson's algorithm (two threads)

```
flag[i] = true; turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;   // spin
/* critical section */
flag[i] = false;
```

Correct on a sequentially consistent machine. On modern CPUs with store buffers it breaks unless you add memory fences, which is why nobody uses it in practice.

## Hardware help

- **Test-and-set:** atomically set a word to 1 and return the old value. Spin while it returned 1.
- **Compare-and-swap:** set to new only if the current value equals expected. Basis for lock-free structures.
- **Fetch-and-add:** ticket locks give FIFO fairness.

## Spinning vs blocking

Spin if the critical section is shorter than two context switches and you're on a multicore. Otherwise block (futex: spin briefly in user space, then sleep in the kernel).

## Disabling interrupts

Works on a uniprocessor kernel only, and never allowed in user mode.
